{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788301238,"nonce":"Q6vGI4yHlSGIEAqKM9+Gn1KhleM=","message":"BOTH HALVES SHIPPED, PLUS 40ed42f PART 3. NOTHING HAS MET A BENCH. Branch\n`spec/advance-halt`, commits `f9f61d2` (source), `71fc15f` (acceptance),\n`1dcdbd0` (blast radius), `64b3840` (drivers + schema), `c32edb8` (DESIGN + a\nmerge fix). `main` merged in at `646cf50`. `dotnet build -c Release`: 0 warnings,\n0 errors. The branch carries no assembly.\n\n## GREP BEFORE BUILD — what I actually found\n\nSession 19's brief listed four blockers as open and one was half-shipped, so I\nchecked both of mine rather than trusting the list. **Neither existed.**\n`grep -rin 'watermark|unread|read_watermark'` over `Source/`, `rwa/`, `accept/`,\n`playbook/`, `checklists/`, `DESIGN.md`, `JOURNAL.md` returned only unrelated\nprose (`ConstructionVerbs`' \"watermarks red errors\", `Siting`'s\n`research-unreadable`, a `journal watermark` note in `accept/8b0b88f`'s own\nrunbook). `grep -rn 'casualty|through_casualties|bleedout-deadline'` over\n`Source/` returned only comments about the M1 deaths — `TriageVerbs`,\n`WorkCoverage`, `DigestVerb`, `SeekVerbs`. The shipped halt reasons were\n`ticks|timeout|interrupted|letter|threat|alert|event|condition|layout|red_error|\ndialog|stalled|exception` and no more. Both halves were genuinely absent.\n\n## HALF 1 — THE CHOICE, AND THE NUMBERS BEHIND IT\n\n**I chose the REFUSAL. The alternative is rejected because it is what already\nfailed.** Step 148 of `m1-20260831` returned `journal_seq:[125,128]` — the\nadvance result CARRIED the news that Table was down — and the run advanced five\nmore times while he bled for 11,335 ticks. Attaching the events themselves is\nthe same fix, larger. An echo a caller may ignore is precisely the failure.\n\nThe bytes are the second argument, measured rather than estimated, over 24\nsession journals on this bench:\n\n| measurement | value |\n|---|---|\n| bytes per event, mean | **243** (2,885 events / 702,393 bytes) |\n| longest single event | **4,416** bytes (`20260901T180111`) |\n| densest sustained run | 166 events / 54,397 bytes over 76,635 ticks = **~1,775 B / 2,500 ticks**, **~42 KB for a 60,000-tick advance** |\n| raid burst | **20,928 bytes in 102 ticks** (`20260901T163220`) |\n| a real `advance` envelope today | **698–1,383 bytes** (67 samples) |\n\nSo attaching grows every advance result **15–60x typically**, on every call\nwhether anything happened or not, bounded in the tail only by the `journal`\nverb's own 2,000-event cap (~8.8 MB). Refusing costs one round trip the loop\nalready owed (`PLAY-LOOP.md` §read step 1 is unconditional).\n\n**THE WINDOW THAT BLOCKS IS THE PREVIOUS ADVANCE'S DELTA, not \"any unread\nevent\".** Your scope says \"has this client read the journal SINCE THE LAST\nADVANCE\", and the distinction is load-bearing. Events emitted while TIME RAN are\nnews nobody saw. Events emitted while the agent is AT THE WHEEL are its own acts,\neach of which already returned a result envelope — and the loop is read → think →\nACT → advance, with every mutating verb writing an `action` row, so blocking on\nthose charges a `journal` round trip to every turn that acted. That is friction\nwith no safety in it, and friction is what makes a run leave the escape on. Two\nconsequences, stated rather than discovered: the FIRST advance of a session is\nnever refused (nothing has run unobserved), and an advance that journaled nothing\ncreates no obligation, so a quiet colony never pays. Still zero judgement — two\nlongs, no allow-list of \"interesting\" types, which is the shape that would have\nhad to GUESS that `downed` mattered.\n\n**THE WATERMARK MOVES ONLY ON `journal`.** Not on the digest —\n`DigestVerb.ChangedSection` publishes `Journal.CountsSince`, a count per type, and\n\"downed: 1\" does not name the pawn, the faction or the tick; the M1 run made ten\ndigest calls. Not on the advance's own echo, for the reason above. Not on a `cat`\nof the file, because the bytes are the same and the mod cannot see a shell. A\nFILTERED or truncated read moves it only as far as the events it handed over, so\n`journal {types:[\"letter\"]}` does not discharge a `downed` it never asked for;\n`unread_after` says what is left.\n\n## WHAT A CLIENT IS — ONE GLOBAL WATERMARK, and why that is honest\n\nInvestigated, not assumed. `Poller.ScanInbox` reads `commands/\u003cid\u003e.json`\nenvelopes carrying `id`, `op` and `args` and nothing else: no handshake, no\nconnect/disconnect edge, no field a client could stamp itself with. `rwa`'s\n`new_id(op)` is `\u003cop\u003e-\u003cHHMMSS\u003e-\u003cpid4\u003e`, fresh for every COMMAND, because each\n`rwa` invocation is its own process — even the pid suffix names a call, not a\nclient. A dictionary keyed on an unvalidated string also has no expiry edge, so\nit is a leak with no way to close it. **I did not invent a registry the bridge\ncannot support.**\n\n**What breaks when a second client appears:** the watermark is shared, so a\n`rwa journal` typed at a shell by a human watching the bench discharges the\nagent's obligation and its next advance proceeds having read nothing. That is a\nreal hazard here — you read the journal mid-run. The mitigation that ships is\nVISIBILITY, not prevention: `journal` publishes `read_watermark`,\n`watermark_was`, `watermark_moved`, `unread_after`, and every advance echoes\n`journal_read_watermark` and `journal_unread`, so a client tracking its own last\nread can see the number move without it. The upgrade, when a second client is\nreal, is an OPTIONAL `client` field on the envelope defaulting to one name — one\nline in `Poller.ScanInbox`, one dictionary beside `Journal.readWatermark`. Named,\nnot built.\n\n## HALF 2 — THE CASUALTY HALT\n\n`reason:\"casualty\"`, through the SHIPPED early-halt path: `Notice` → `Halt` →\n`Finish`, so `halted_on` and `halted_seq` are the same fields\n`until:{condition|layout}` already populates. No second envelope.\n\n`halted_on` = `{kind:\"casualty\", event:\"downed\"|\"death\", pawn, pawn_id, faction,\nplayer_faction:true, pawn_kind, tick, damage?, detail}`.\n\n**Default-on and NOT a matcher.** `until:{event:{type:\"downed\"}}` always existed\nand the run did not use it; the failure was the DEFAULT. Opt-in would ship the\nfix switched off.\n\n**THE FILTER IS ON FACTION, and it is provable.** `Verse/Faction.IsPlayer`\n(`def.isPlayer`), resolved on the MAIN thread by the emitting hook and carried in\nthe payload as `player`, because `TimeDriver.Notice` is documented \"any thread,\ncalled synchronously from Journal.Emit\" and may not touch Verse. Deliberately NOT\n`Verse/Pawn.IsColonist` (`Faction.IsPlayer \u0026\u0026 RaceProps.Humanlike \u0026\u0026 (!IsSlave ||\nguest.SlaveIsSecure)`), which excludes an INSECURE slave — precisely a casualty\nsomebody must look at. `kind` (colonist|slave|animal|mech) rides beside it so the\nnarrower reading is the caller's to take. Phases 3 and 4 of the suite are one\ncontrolled experiment: same fixture step, same predicate, same span, faction the\nonly variable.\n\nBoth hooks are on the TRANSITION (`MakeDowned`, `SetDead`), so a pawn already\ndown when an advance starts emits neither — the halt cannot re-fire for the same\npawn and cannot wedge.\n\n## 40ed42f PART 3 — AND IT CONSUMES `triage`\n\n`error.code:\"bleedout-deadline\"`. I extracted `CasualtyRow` out of `Triage`'s\nloop so the verb and the refusal share **one pathfind and one verdict**; the\nrefusal reads `verdict == \"too-slow\"` off a row `triage` itself would publish and\nrecomputes nothing. `PawnSerializer.BleedoutBlock` stays the single clock builder.\n\n**IT REFUSES ON `too-slow` AND ON NOTHING ELSE — this is your bench finding,\ndecided.** `no-rescuer`, `no-path` and `no-deadline` do not refuse. The refusal\nexists to force ONE decision into the open (\"this pawn dies unless you act\"), and\nthat decision only exists when there is a TIMING question. Where nobody can reach\nthe patient at all, no act clears the condition, the next advance refuses\nidentically, and a ten-day unattended run stops dead on a state it cannot fix —\na wall, not a guard. Your `accept/runs/s21-20260901/` case is exactly why: no bed\non a `--quicktest` map → `TakeToBedGate` → `HealthAIUtility.CanRescueNow` →\n`WantsToBeRescued` answers `no-bed` for everyone → every verdict `no-rescuer`,\nnull margin. A refusal keyed on \"somebody is bleeding\" would have made `advance`\nunusable on the standard bench. The casualty is not lost by staying quiet: the\nadvance HALTED when they went down, the clock is in every `pawn` read, and\n`triage` names the gate that refused each candidate — `no-bed` says build a bed,\nwhich is an act, where \"refuse to advance\" says nothing.\n\n`WantsToBeRescued`'s first two clauses (`!Downed`, `InBed()`) also put a STANDING\nbleeder and a BEDDED bleeder outside this refusal, correctly: a patient in bed\nwhose DOCTOR cannot arrive in time is a different comparison (tend travel, not\nrescue travel) and I do not claim it.\n\n**COST, AND WHEN IT IS SKIPPED.** Gated cheapest-first, once at ARM, never per\nframe or per tick:\n\n1. one `FreeColonistsSpawned` snapshot;\n2. per colonist, ONE CACHED FLOAT — `Verse/HediffSet.BleedRateTotal` returns\n   `cachedBleedRate` and recomputes only when the game dirtied it. **Nobody\n   bleeding costs exactly this and stops here: no pathfind, no hediff walk, no\n   `ShouldBeTendedNowByPlayer`;**\n3. only for a bleeder: the clock (`TicksUntilDeathDueToBloodLoss`, one division)\n   and, only if FINITE, the full row — where the `FindPathNow` calls live, capped\n   at 3 per patient.\n\nWorst case: 3 pathfinds per BLEEDING patient, once per advance.\n\n## `ok:false` — YOUR SESSION-13 REASONING, REPRODUCED IN THE CODE\n\nBoth refusal codes carry the argument at the declaration site. A refused\n`dev:spawn-thing` is `ok:true` because it is an ANSWER ABOUT THE WORLD — a\nwidget-gate answer, information (PLAY-LOOP §act). These are the mod REFUSING TO\nACT: no ticks ran, nothing armed, the clock untouched, and a caller branching on\n`ok` alone must land in its error path rather than read a `data` block and\nconclude time passed. A failed envelope carries no `data`\n(`Poller.BuildResultJson`), so every number is in `error.detail` as `key=value`\ntokens — `unread=`, `unread_total=`, `read_watermark=`, `advance_end_seq=`,\n`bleedout_ticks=`, `rescue_ticks=`, `margin_ticks=` — parseable as well as\nreadable. The unread detail also carries the TYPE BREAKDOWN (`types: downed 1,\nletter 1, alert_on 2`), which is the line that would have said Table's name.\n\n## THE ESCAPES — AND WHAT AN OPT-OUT COSTS\n\n`advance {unread_ok:\"\u003cwhy\u003e\"}` and `advance {through_casualties:\"\u003cwhy\u003e\"}`, exactly\nas ruled. Required non-empty string; empty, whitespace and non-string are all\n`bad-args`. One `action` row per advance (`verb:\"advance\"`, `step:\"escape\"`, the\nreasons, a `bypassed` list naming what it actually overrode) plus an envelope echo\n(`unread_ok`, `through_casualties`, `escaped`).\n\n**What it costs, in my words: the escape IS the discipline made optional.** A run\nthat passes it on every advance has exactly the mod it had before this issue, and\nno amount of refusal design prevents that. What the three controls buy is that it\nbecomes EXPENSIVE and VISIBLE rather than impossible:\n\n1. **Per-call, never a mode.** No session flag, no config key, no environment\n   variable — \"turn it off and forget\" is not spellable. And `unread_ok`\n   deliberately does not move the watermark, so it buys ONE call and the next\n   advance asks again: three in-game days of riding past a delta costs three\n   separate journaled admissions.\n2. **A required reason.** The argument cannot degrade into a bare boolean, and\n   the reason is the thing a post-mortem actually reads.\n3. **A greppable row, in two places.** `journal --types action` finds every use;\n   the envelope echo means a transcript-only audit finds them too.\n\n**Are three enough? No — and the gap is precise.** They buy DETECTABILITY, not\nprevention: nothing FAILS a run for standing on the escape. The two additions I\nwould make are a per-run BUDGET (N escaped advances, then the mod refuses the\nescape itself) and a hard `advance-discipline` FAIL above a fraction of escaped\nadvances. Both need a threshold, no number is defensible before the M1 re-run\nproduces one, and a guessed threshold is the thing this project keeps deleting —\nso the measurement ships first and the wall waits for evidence. `4.2`'s WARN with\nthe reason verbatim is the measurement.\n\n## ACCEPTANCE — WHAT IS NOW PROVABLE, AND HOW\n\n`accept/722c951-advance-halt.py`, 87 checks / 7 phases, plus\n`accept/4.2-play-loop.py`'s new `advance-discipline`. I added a suite rather than\nonly extending 4.2 because 4.2 is a POST-HOC run auditor that sends no commands —\nit cannot exercise a refusal — and 722c951's last bullet is about 4.2's ability\nto TELL the returns apart, which is an auditor job. Both, doing their own half.\n\n| your bullet | where | how |\n|---|---|---|\n| two advances, no read → second refused | 1.5 | `ok:false`, code `unread-journal` |\n| the refusal names how many | 1.6–1.9 | `unread=\u003cN\u003e` token, seq range, `types:` breakdown |\n| an advance AFTER a read proceeds (watermark MOVES) | 1.13–1.17 | `watermark_moved:true`, `unread_after:0`, then `ok:true` on the same call |\n| the escape exists, is explicit, is journalled | 2.5–2.12, 5.1–5.6 | envelope echo + an `action` row with the reason verbatim |\n| own-faction downing halts with pawn/event/tick | 3.3–3.14 | `reason:\"casualty\"`, `halted_on.{pawn,pawn_id,event,tick,pawn_kind}` |\n| a HOSTILE downing does NOT halt | 4.2–4.8 | same fixture, same predicate, same span; `reason:\"condition\"`, and the `downed` row shows `player:false` |\n| the bleedout refusal reports both numbers | 6.2–6.8 | `bleedout_ticks` / `rescue_ticks` / `margin_ticks` matched against `triage`'s own row |\n| the M1 six-advance shape is unexpressible | 1.5, 1.11, 3.15–3.17 | the second blind advance is refused; a casualty halt lands within one frame of the downing and its own row then blocks the next advance |\n| 4.2 tells the returns apart | `advance-discipline` | blind refusal FAILs, casualty halt ridden past FAILs, zero-`journal`-calls FAILs, bleedout refusal WARNs, escapes WARN with their reason |\n\n**Extras I added because the gap was visible:** `bad-args` on an empty /\nwhitespace / non-string reason (2.2–2.4); a DIGEST does not discharge the delta\n(1.11); a types-FILTERED read does not discharge what it never asked for\n(2.13–2.16); the escape is not a mode — the advance after an escaped one is\nrefused again (2.17–2.18); `through_casualties` also covers the bleedout refusal\n(6.9–6.12).\n\n**NO BARE TICK COUNTS.** Every timed advance is bounded by\n`until:{condition:{path:\"time.tick\", op:\"\u003e=\", value:\u003cabsolute tick\u003e}}` — \"until\nthe clock reaches T\", T read off the game. That is also what makes the halt\nassertions sharp: the advance either stops EARLY on the casualty or runs on to\n`reason:\"condition\"`, and the reason token is the whole discriminator. Phase 1 is\nthe stated exception, with the reason in a comment: it needs the journal to GROW\nduring an advance, \"wait until something is journaled\" has no predicate spelling,\nand it uses the shipped `letter_delay_ticks` fixture with `until:{letter:true}` —\nthe play loop's own standing guard — rather than a number I invented. Shape\nassertions use key EXISTENCE (`shape()` / `has_key`), including three ABSENCE\nassertions (`unread_ok`/`escaped` absent when no escape was used; no `data` on a\nrefusal), because `eq(..., None)` passes on an absent key.\n\n`advance-discipline`'s verdicts are split on WHO COULD HAVE KNOWN: a blind\nadvance FAILs (§read step 1 is unconditional and was skipped); a bleedout refusal\nWARNs (the loop could not have known without running a pathfinder, so being told\nis the mod working); re-advancing straight off a casualty halt FAILs. Plus the\nnumber nothing measured before: advancing at all with ZERO `journal` ops. **Run\nagainst the real transcript it FAILs `transcripts/m1-20260831` on \"29 advances\nand ZERO `journal` calls\"** — the historic run, caught by the auditor that could\nnot see it. Nine mutations and two WARN-surface assertions each prove their own\ncheck can fail; the clean fixture stays green.\n\n## THE FIXTURE, DECLARED\n\n`journal-selftest --steps down-at {down_pawn?, down_delay_ticks?, down_kill?}`.\nDeclared as an undeclared addition, dev-gated, journaled as a `dev` event,\nsuperseded by 3.1 — the same discipline as `error-at`. **It is the only thing\nthat can drive this acceptance:** every other route to a downing (`dev:damage`,\nthe existing `downed` step) fires from the command drain with the game PAUSED,\nwhich produces the event but never INSIDE an advance. Armed here, fired from\n`GameComponentTick` — inside `DoSingleTick`, inside the advance — through the\ngame's own `HealthUtility.DamageUntilDowned`/`DamageUntilDead`, so the journal\nhooks see exactly what a real casualty produces. `down_pawn` takes a\nthingIDNumber, so the SAME step proves both halves of the faction filter.\n\nOne line added to `AgentGameComponent.GameComponentTick` beside\n`TickErrorFixture`, and one to `GameBoundary` so a fixture armed against one\ncolony cannot fire against the next.\n\n## THE BLAST RADIUS\n\nEleven executable suites and four hand-driven runbooks, found with\n`grep -n 'advance' accept/*`. **One wrapper per suite, never an escape at a call\nsite** — a `unread_ok` sprinkled inline is indistinguishable to the next reader\nfrom one somebody added to get a red check green; in one place, with the file's\nown name as the reason, `journal --types action` says WHICH harness turned the\nguard off. Rewritten: `1.8-game-clock-advance.sh` (10 sites, via a jq injector),\n`3.4-pawn-orders.{py,ps1}` (8 each), `3.5-dialog-verbs.{py,ps1}` (5 each),\n`fc287ba-until-state.py` (13), `s13-mod-surface.py` (its existing wrapper),\n`4087644-order-honesty.py` (3), `70ac258-things-stable-order.py`,\n`3.6-bills-storage.py`, `32b9e01-orders-makingfor.ps1`. The four `.md` runbooks\nget a note at the top. `accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.*` untouched — worker A's.\n\nfc287ba's phase-0 `bad-args` checks still hold: `until` is parsed before the\nescapes are.\n\nDrivers: `playbook/PLAY-LOOP.md` (two pre-advance gates + a `casualty` row in the\nHalts table), `checklists/triggered.md` (a new `casualty-halt` item — read\n`halted_on`, then `triage`, then send its `act`, because `rescue` was shipped and\ncalled ZERO times in 195 ops), `M1-PROMPT.md` (both gates in the audited rules,\nand \"every use of the escape will be read in the post-mortem\"), `rwa/README.md`\n(the three non-matcher behaviours, the token contract, `:str` for the reason\nflags, and that `rwa replay` deliberately does NOT inject the escapes — replaying\n`m1-20260831` shows the refusal firing on the advance after Table went down),\n`JOURNAL.md` (the payload and result-shape changes, including that tailing the\nfile moves nothing).\n\n## THE MERGE FOUND A LATENT DEFECT — worth your attention\n\n`main` merged with ZERO textual conflicts and one SEMANTIC one that git could not\nsee. `spec/arg-names` gave `journal-selftest` a `RefuseStray` guard over the\n`SelftestArgs` allowlist; this branch gave it the `down-at` step. Different lines,\nso both merged silently — and the result would have **refused every `down-at`\ncall BEFORE any step ran**, which is the fixture phases 3, 4 and 5 are built on\nand the one phase 0's precondition probes. Fixed in `c32edb8`:\n`down_delay_ticks`, `down_pawn`, `down_kill` added, with a comment saying that an\nallowlist is exactly the thing two branches can each be separately right about.\n**Any other branch adding a `journal-selftest` step in this run has the same\nlatent break.**\n\n## THE ARG-NAMES INTERACTION — the report still reaches the journal\n\n`advance`'s `ignored_args` cannot ride the envelope (it returns\n`DeferredResult`), and worker D already branches on that: for a deferred verb the\nreport goes out via `Log.Warning`, which `JournalHooks.Patch_LogWarning` turns\ninto a `warning` row. **My change does not disturb it.** Both my refusals return\na `Result` from `TimeDriver.Start`, and `TimeVerbs.Advance` enqueues it and STILL\nreturns `DeferredResult.Instance` — so `Execute` takes the same deferred branch\nand the stray report still reaches the journal. Verified by reading the path, not\nby running it.\n\nI also MOVED both refusals to sit BELOW the speed-argument reads: returning from\nabove them would leave `speed`/`max_tps` unqueried on a refused advance and make\nthe caller's own arguments look ignored — a refusal manufacturing a second, false\ncomplaint. They still sit ABOVE `SetSpeed`, so a refusal leaves the clock exactly\nwhere it found it. The escape's journal row moved to AFTER the speed took, so an\nadvance the game itself refused (`cannot-set-speed`) does not leave a confession\nto a decision nobody got to make. **The escapes need no registration anywhere** —\nreading them through `ctx.Args` marks them read, which is the whole design.\n\n## NOT DONE / BLOCKED\n\n- **Nothing has met a bench.** No verb here has run against a live game. You own\n  every in-game acceptance; I launched nothing.\n- **The three `.ps1` suites are NOT machine-checked** — `pwsh` is not installed\n  on this box and they run on BORGES. The edit is mechanical (one wrapper,\n  `Send-Cmd advance` → `Send-Advance` at every site) but the syntax is unverified.\n- **Phase 6 needs a bed and some distance.** On a bedless `--quicktest` map it\n  exits 2 with both known gaps named in the SKIP text. Not a spec failure.\n- Not blocked on anything, and I needed no change in `DigestVerb.cs`,\n  `SeekVerbs.cs` or `VerbRegistry.cs`.","files":null}]}